  "title": "So-called Early Christian Basilica",
  "provenance": "Pleiades",
  "placeTypeURIs": [
    "https://pleiades.stoa.org/vocabularies/place-types/basilica"
  ],
  "details": "<p>The basilica was most likely built in the fourth century A.D. It has an entrance to the east, a single nave, and a semicircular apse. On the floor of the nave, two tombs are visible, possibly belonging to the martyrs Libertino and Peregrino.</p>",
  "review_state": "published",
  "type": "FeatureCollection",
  "description": "A small basilica located outside the walls of the ancient Greek city of Akragas, at the foot of the so-called Temple of Juno, on the eastern slopes of the Hill of the Temples, in an area previously occupied by the necropolis of the late Roman period.",
